'''''Umbilicus horizontalis''''', the '''horizontal navelwort''', is a fleshy perennial [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Crassulaceae]] (in the [[genus]] ''[[Umbilicus (plant)|Umbilicus]]'') native to the [[Mediterranean Basin]] and [[Middle East]], from the [[Azores]] in the west to [[Oman]] in the east<ref>{{cite web |title=Umbilicus horizontalis (Guss.) DC. |url=https://www.catalogueoflife.org/data/taxon/7DGFJ |publisher=[[Catalogue of Life]] |access-date=24 March 2021}}</ref> in rocky habitats.

Horizontal navelwort grows to an average of {{convert|25|cm|in|round=0.5|abbr=on}} high. The thickly clustered, bell-shaped flowers are pale green, and grow in a [[raceme]] perpendicular to the spike, unlike [[Umbilicus rupestris|common navelwort]], whose flowers droop (thus the term "horizontal").

The plant often grows on shady walls or in damp rock crevices that are sparse in other plant growth, where its succulent, dark green leaves develop in [[rosette (botany)|rosette]]s that are about {{convert|7|cm|in|round=0.5|abbr=on}} wide. Horizontal navelwort is similar to common navelwort in many respects, but is noticeably more succulent.

